  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'State Kepler''s laws of planetary motion. (3 marks)', '1. Law of orbits: every planet moves around the sun in an elliptical orbit, with the sun at one of the two foci of the ellipse.
2. Law of areas: the line joining a planet to the sun sweeps out equal areas in equal intervals of time. So a planet moves fastest when it is nearest the sun (perihelion) and slowest when farthest (aphelion) - a result of conservation of angular momentum.
3. Law of periods: the square of the time a planet takes to go round the sun (T) is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis of its orbit (a): T² ∝ a³.', null, '[{"point": "First law (law of orbits): every planet moves in an elliptical orbit with the sun at one focus", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Second law (law of areas): the line joining the planet and the sun sweeps out equal areas in equal intervals of time (planet moves faster when nearer the sun)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Third law (law of periods): the square of the orbital period is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis, T² ∝ a³", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
